To deploy a Next.js e-commerce website completely for free, you need a free hosting provider for your frontend, a free serverless database for your data, and a free tier payment/auth infrastructure.The absolute best and easiest way to achieve this is by using Vercel for hosting, GitHub for version control, and Stripe (in test mode) for checkout management.📦 Step 1: Prep and Push Your Code to GitHubYour Next.js project must be stored in a remote repository before it can be deployed to a free cloud platform.Initialize Git in your project folder: git initStage all files: git add .Commit your changes: git commit -m "Ready for deploy"Create a free repository on GitHub and push your code:bashgit remote add origin <your-github-repo-url>
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main
Use code with caution.🌐 Step 2: Deploy Free Hosting on VercelVercel is the creator of Next.js and offers a robust, free Hobby Tier that natively optimizes Next.js features like Server Actions and partial rendering.Go to Vercel and sign up using your GitHub account.Click "Add New" and select "Project".Import your newly created e-commerce repository.Expand the Environment Variables tab. Do not miss this step: Paste all the required .env keys from your payment gateway, database, or CMS here.Click "Deploy". Vercel will give you a live vercel.app URL for free in less than two minutes.🔋 Step 3: Utilize Free E-Commerce InfrastructureTo keep the website completely free to run, pair your Vercel deployment with these zero-cost backend services:Database: Use Neon PostgreSQL or Supabase. Both offer generous free tiers perfect for storing your user profiles, product catalogs, and order tables.Payments: Use Stripe. While building and testing your app, Stripe test mode is entirely free. They only charge a transaction fee when you make real, live sales.Authentication: Use Clerk Auth or Auth.js. Clerk gives you up to 10,000 monthly active users completely free.🏗️ Alternative Free Hosting PlatformsIf you prefer not to use Vercel, you can deploy your Next.js frontend to these alternative platforms:Netlify: Excellent free tier that supports Next.js routing and cache control utilities seamlessly.Render: Best if you export your e-commerce platform as a static site (output: 'export' inside your next.config.js). You can host it free using Render Static Sites.